\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


For a positive integer $a$, let $R(a)=\prod_{p\mid a}p$ be its radical, with $R(1)=1$.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Does there exist an integer $k\ge1$ such that for every $m,n\ge1$, equality $R(m+i)=R(n+i)$ for all $0\le i<k$ implies $m=n$? \href{https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0312440}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Can a fixed number of successive prime-divisor sets uniquely determine the starting positive integer?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{description}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems, record 8700010 (AMR-086-0010). CC BY 4.0. \url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] Michel Waldschmidt, Open Diophantine Problems, arXiv:math/0312440v2 (24 January 2004), Conjecture 2.2. \url{https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0312440}.
\end{description}
